  "body": "## Description and Impact\n\nIn toPackedFloat we represent floating point numbers with mantissa size either 38 or 72 depending upon the floating point size. If the mantissa is not exactly 38 or 72 digit long we normalize it, i.e, convert it to either 38  digit or 72 digit long mantissa. And to decide whether the given input mantissa should be normalized to M_MANTISSA (38) or L_MANTISSA (72) we use a `isResultL` bool that checks if the `MAXIMUM_EXPONENT` is signed less than the `mantissaMultiplier + exponent`. But this logic for calculating the bool will result in huge precision loss for floating point numbers that have digits > 38 but the exponent is quite less than the `MAXIMUM_EXPONENT` that the `isResultL` calculation always results in false and those numbers will be wrongly stored in M_MANTISSA range instead of the L_MANTISSA range. This results in a significant precision loss. To further confirm this issue the sponsor has confirmed that floating points with 38 < digits < 72 are valid inputs for this library as seen [here](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/00924b7c-9ed2-46d8-8b3f-da9495b36626)\n\n## Mitigation\n\nI could not think of a detailed mitigation since submitting it last moment, but we need to factor in the `digitsMantissa` too during the formulation of `isResultL` to correctly classify which floating point numbers require L_MANTISSA and which require M_MANTISSA",
  "proof_of_concept": "## Proof of Concept\n\nAdded a few console.logs in the `toPackedFloat` function of the Source code for verbosity. Inside of the normalisation flow.\n\n```diff\n            if (\n                !((mantissa <= int(MAX_M_DIGIT_NUMBER) && mantissa >= int(MIN_M_DIGIT_NUMBER)) ||\n                    (mantissa <= int(MAX_L_DIGIT_NUMBER) && mantissa >= int(MIN_L_DIGIT_NUMBER)))\n            ) {\n                digitsMantissa = findNumberOfDigits(uint(mantissa));\n+               console.log(\"digitsMantissa : \", digitsMantissa);\n                bool isResultL;\n                assembly {\n                    //p this calc process ensures that for mantissa digits < M_MANTISSA range we normalize it to M_MANTISSA\n                    mantissaMultiplier := sub(digitsMantissa, MAX_DIGITS_M)//p it overflows no safeguards \n                    isResultL := slt(MAXIMUM_EXPONENT, add(exponent, mantissaMultiplier))\n                    //p if we need to normalize it to L_MANTISSA\n                    if isResultL {\n                        mantissaMultiplier := sub(mantissaMultiplier, DIGIT_DIFF_L_M)\n                        float := or(float, MANTISSA_L_FLAG_MASK)//@q are we setting the L FLAG for MANTISSA here?\n                    }\n                    exponent := add(exponent, mantissaMultiplier)\n                    let negativeMultiplier := and(TWO_COMPLEMENT_SIGN_MASK, mantissaMultiplier)\n                    if negativeMultiplier {\n                        mantissa := mul(mantissa, exp(BASE, sub(0, mantissaMultiplier)))\n                    }\n                    if iszero(negativeMultiplier) {\n                        mantissa := div(mantissa, exp(BASE, mantissaMultiplier))\n                    }\n                }\n+               console.log(\"mantissa multiplier :\", mantissaMultiplier);\n+               console.log(\"isResultL :\", isResultL);\n```\nThen create a test file and paste this Test code. \n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {Float128, packedFloat} from \"../src/Float128.sol\";\nimport {Test, console} from \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\n\ncontract LibraryUser is Test {\nusing Float128 for int256;\n\n    function testConvertToFloat() public pure {\n        int256 mantissa = 12345678912345678912345678912345678921548654612; //47 digit number\n        int256 exponent = -32; //123456789123456.78912345678912345678921548654612\n        packedFloat float = mantissa.toPackedFloat(exponent);\n        console.log(\"Float returned after toPackedFloat : \", packedFloat.unwrap(float));\n        (mantissa, exponent) = Float128.decode(float);\n        console.log(\"Mantissa and Exponent : \");\n        console.logInt(mantissa);\n        console.logInt(exponent);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nRun the test with `forge test --mt testConvertToFloat -vv`\n\nWe get the following logs:\n```\n[PASS] testConvertToFloat() (gas: 9424)\nLogs:\n  digitsMantissa :  47\n  mantissa multiplier : 9\n  isResultL : false\n  Float returned after toPackedFloat :  57733494688698486353463829134275605423191209493220070754493949912349989479497\n  Mantissa and Exponent : \n  12345678912345678912345678912345678921\n  -23\n```\n**Actual Number**: 123456789123456.78912345678912345678921548654612\\\n**Library Number**  : 123456789123456.78912345678912345678921\n\nHere we see our number has 47 digits so this will enter the normalisation flow. And the exponent is -32. But since the `mantissaMultiplier` will not overflow as the `digitsMantissa` is more thn 38. This will result in `isResultL` to be wrongly calculated due to the exponent being very lower than -18. The `add(exponent, mantissaMultiplier)` will result in a number lesser than -18. making `isResultL` to be false and setting the mantissa length to medium. \n\n**So basically in scenarios where the floating point number digits is more than 38 but the `exponent` is lesser than -18 such that the addition of `exponent` and `mantissaMultiplier` result in a value lesser than -18 will result in `isResultL` to be false which will set the total digits for mantissa to be in the medium range resulting in loss of decimal digits**",
